# XSD Appendix G `quantifier` (`?`, `*`, `+`, `{n,m}`, `{n,}`, `{n}`) and the
# reluctant (`?`-suffixed) forms XPath F&O 3.1 5.6.1 adds to that grammar.

# {n,m}: between n and m occurrences, inclusive.
^a{2,3}$	-	match	aa
^a{2,3}$	-	match	aaa
^a{2,3}$	-	nomatch	a
^a{2,3}$	-	nomatch	aaaa

# {n,}: n or more occurrences.
^a{2,}$	-	match	aa
^a{2,}$	-	match	aaaa
^a{2,}$	-	nomatch	a

# {n}: exactly n occurrences.
^a{2}$	-	match	aa
^a{2}$	-	nomatch	a
^a{2}$	-	nomatch	aaa

# Reluctant quantifiers: `?` after `+`, `?` or `{n,m}` changes where the engine
# prefers to stop, not which strings are in the language, so the same subjects
# must still match. Regexes without the `?` suffix are pinned above.
^a+?b$	-	match	ab
^a+?b$	-	match	aab
^a+?b$	-	nomatch	b
^a??$	-	match	a
^a??$	-	nomatch	b
^a{2,3}?$	-	match	aa
^a{2,3}?$	-	match	aaa
^a{2,3}?$	-	nomatch	a
^a{2,3}?$	-	nomatch	aaaa

# A quantified group: the quantifier applies to the whole group.
^(ab)*$	-	match	ab
^(ab)*$	-	match	abab
^(ab)*$	-	nomatch	aba

# `\{` is a SingleCharEsc spelling a literal brace, so this is the four
# characters `a{2}` and not the quantifier `a{2}`.
^a\{2\}$	-	match	a{2}
^a\{2\}$	-	nomatch	aa
